// aliasing.bs -- "naming is aliasing" (ticket 09 §1), and the inline spelling
// that had to exist before that sentence was true (ticket 68 Q7, ENG-331).
//
//     bsc --src-root examples examples/Aliasing ViaAlias "(:ok, 7)"      -> 7
//     bsc --src-root examples examples/Aliasing Inline "(:error, :nope)" -> 0
//     bsc --src-root examples examples/Aliasing Pick 1                   -> :ok
//
// THE PROPERTY WORTH LOOKING AT is that `ViaAlias` and `Inline` below are the
// same function. Ticket 09 §1 argues that a `type` declaration introduces no
// new type -- it names one that already exists -- and it showed the two
// spellings side by side to make the point. The second one did not parse.
// `param`, `signature` and `foreign_sig` each took a `type_prim`, and only
// `type_expr` reached the union rule, so a union was writable inside a tuple,
// a record field or a generic argument, but not in the position a reader tries
// first. The argument for naming-is-aliasing was written in a syntax the
// language did not have.
module Aliasing

type Fetched = (:ok, int) | (:error, atom)

// THE NAMED SPELLING.
public int ViaAlias(Fetched f)

ViaAlias((:ok, n))    -> n
ViaAlias((:error, _)) -> 0

// THE INLINE SPELLING, AND IT IS THE SAME TYPE. The same clause heads
// discriminate it, the same exhaustiveness check proves it, and deleting a
// clause produces the same residual. Nothing about the union reaches the
// checker differently: `resolve` sees a `t_union` whichever way it was
// written, which is why this was a grammar change and nothing else.
public int Inline((:ok, int) | (:error, atom) f)

Inline((:ok, n))    -> n
Inline((:error, _)) -> 0

// A RETURN POSITION TAKES ONE TOO. Ticket 68 Q7 answered (b) -- all three
// positions -- rather than parameters alone, because (a) leaves the reader a
// rule with nothing behind it but the order the grammar happened to be written
// in: `Handle(:ok | :error x)` legal and `:ok | :error Pick(int)` a syntax
// error.
public :ok | :error Pick(int n)

Pick(n) when n > 0  -> :ok
Pick(n) when n <= 0 -> :error

// AN UNMARKED SIGNATURE IS PRIVATE (F12), so here the union sits between
// nothing at all and the function name. Q7 weighed this as the hardest place
// in a C-family declaration to scan, and took it anyway rather than ship the
// asymmetry.
int Score(:ok | :error r)

Score(:ok)    -> 1
Score(:error) -> 0

public int Rate(int n)

Rate(n) -> Score(Pick(n))
